  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "dsh-auth-everying",
      "text": "`dsh-auth-everying` is a DeepSeek Harness plugin for importing local coding-platform login state and provider configuration. It lets you choose which imported models appear in the DSH model picker."
    },
    {
      "section": "Features",
      "text": "- Imports local Claude, Codex, Grok, Gemini, Copilot, OpenCode, and CC Switch configuration.\n- Supports OAuth login for compatible official providers.\n- Discovers models from OpenAI-compatible CC Switch gateways through `/v1/models` or `/models`.\n- Exposes the Codex app-compatible `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, and `max` levels without changing their wire values. The unsupported `minimal` level is hidden for CC Switch Codex routes.\n- Uses `medium` as the concrete Codex default so DSH does not add its separate `Default` provider-default option.\n- Adds `ultra` only to models that declare or are known to support it. Non-reasoning and unsupported models do not show that selector.\n- Preserves the configured Codex `model_reasoning_effort` and sends `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, `max`, and `ultra` with the same wire value.\n- Supports Windows installation and build workflows."
    },
    {
      "section": "Requirements",
      "text": "- A working DeepSeek Harness installation with the `dsh` command available in PowerShell or a terminal.\n- A web profile, because the settings panel is provided by the DSH web interface.\n- Node.js 22.19 or later only when building the project from source."
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "After the package is published to npm, the shortest installation command is:\n\n```sh\ndsh plugin --profile web add dsh-auth-everying\n```\n\nUntil the first npm release is published, install directly from the public GitHub repository:\n\nRun these commands in PowerShell or a terminal:\n\n```sh\ndsh plugin --profile web add github:chenbin-dev/dsh-auth-everying\ndsh web\n```"
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "The package name and the GitHub repository are different distribution channels. Publishing the repository does not by itself publish the npm package.\n\nIf DSH is already running, restart `dsh web` after installing or updating the plugin so the new bundle is loaded."
    },
    {
      "section": "First-Time Setup",
      "text": "1. Open the DSH web interface and go to **Settings > dsh-auth-everying**.\n2. In **Sources**, select the local login or provider configuration to import.\n3. Click the import action and wait for the scan to finish.\n4. In **Imported**, enable the models that should be available in DSH.\n5. Start a new conversation or refresh the model picker."
    },
    {
      "section": "First-Time Setup",
      "text": "The plugin reads local configuration from common locations, including CC Switch, Codex, Claude, Grok, Gemini, and OpenCode. It does not require copying API keys into the project or into the README."
    },
    {
      "section": "CLI",
      "text": "Show available sources and imported routes:\n\n```sh\ndsh plugin --profile web exec dsh-auth-everying status\n```\n\nImport sources by ID after checking `status`:\n\n```sh\ndsh plugin --profile web exec dsh-auth-everying import live:codex-auth live:grok-auth\n```\n\nUse the source IDs reported by `status` when your local installation exposes different IDs."
    },
    {
      "section": "The plugin does not appear in Settings",
      "text": "Confirm that the install command completed successfully, stop the existing `dsh web` process, and start it again. Also verify that the command uses the `web` profile."
    },
    {
      "section": "The imported route has no models",
      "text": "Open the plugin settings and run the source scan again. For a CC Switch OpenAI-compatible provider, confirm that its gateway is reachable and supports either `/v1/models` or `/models`. The configured default model is retained when the gateway does not provide a model list."
    },
    {
      "section": "The reasoning levels do not match the provider",
      "text": "For CC Switch Codex routes, the selector matches the Codex app: `low`, `medium`, `high`, `xhigh`, and `max` are available, while `minimal` is hidden. The plugin uses `medium` as the concrete default, so DSH does not add its separate `Default` entry. `ultra` is model-specific: the plugin reads optional reasoning capability metadata from the gateway and otherwise enables it only for the known GPT-5.6 Codex aliases. Models without that capability, such as image or automatic-review models, do not show `ultra`. Each selected level is sent with the same wire value."
    },
    {
      "section": "Windows installation or startup fails",
      "text": "Use Node.js 22.19 or later for source builds, restart DSH after installation, and ensure the `dsh` command is available in the same terminal where the plugin command is run."
    },
    {
      "section": "Development",
      "text": "```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/chenbin-dev/dsh-auth-everying.git\ncd dsh-auth-everying\nnpm ci\nnpm run check\n\n`npm run check` runs TypeScript checks, tests, and the production build.\n\nDo not commit authentication files, local databases, API keys, tokens, certificates, or `.env` files. Test fixtures must use placeholder credentials only."
    },
    {
      "section": "License",
      "text": "Apache-2.0. See [LICENSE](LICENSE).\n\nProject repository: <https://github.com/chenbin-dev/dsh-auth-everying>"
    }
